{"tool_name":"esefinder","contents":["oeb","bioschemas","biotools"],"fetched_metadata":{"biotools":{"id":"esefinder","home":"http://rulai.cshl.edu/tools/ESE/","license":"Unlicense","summary":"Web-based resource for identifing putative ESEs (exonic splicing enhancers), cis-acting motifs responsible for enhancing splicing.","addition_date":"2017-02-10T11:35:56Z","last_update_date":"2020-06-16T10:55:22Z","tool_type":["Web application"],"version":["3.0"],"collections":["Rare Disease"],"accessibility":"Open access","biotools_curie":"biotools:esefinder","cost":"Free of charge","credit":[{"email":"klin@cshl.edu","name":"Woody Lin","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Primary contact"]}],"documentation":[{"type":["General"],"url":"http://krainer01.cshl.edu/cgi-bin/tools/ESE3/esefinder.cgi?process=background"}],"edit_permission":{"type":"private"},"function":[{"operation":[{"term":"Exonic splicing enhancer prediction","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_0446"},{"term":"Sequence merging","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_0232"},{"term":"Splicing model analysis","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_2499"}]}],"maturity":"Mature","name":"ESEfinder","operating_system":["Linux","Mac","Windows"],"owner":"ELIXIR-EE","publication":[{"doi":"10.1093/nar/gkg616","metadata":{"abstract":"Point mutations frequently cause genetic diseases by disrupting the correct pattern of pre-mRNA splicing. The effect of a point mutation within a coding sequence is traditionally attributed to the deduced change in the corresponding amino acid. However, some point mutations can have much more severe effects on the structure of the encoded protein, for example when they inactivate an exonic splicing enhancer (ESE), thereby resulting in exon skipping. ESEs also appear to be especially important in exons that normally undergo alternative splicing. Different classes of ESE consensus motifs have been described, but they are not always easily identified. ESEfinder (http://exon.cshl.edu/ESE/) is a 